1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,060
Using language, reading, standard English and dialects.

2
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:09,240
What is standard English?

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,080
Is it spoken in Britain, the US,

4
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:15,300
Canada, Australia, India and New Zealand?

5
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:19,920
Believe it or not, there is no such thing as standard English.

6
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:25,280
Many people believe the English spoken on TV and the radio is standard English.

7
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:28,860
This is because in the early days of radio,

8
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:32,880
those who reported the news were expected to speak excellent English.

9
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:38,920
However, on TV and the radio you will hear differences in the way people speak.

10
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:13,180
Geography also plays a part in making dialects.

11
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:17,280
Some people who live in the mountains of the eastern USA speak

12
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:19,340
with an older kind of English dialect.

13
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:22,900
When Americans moved from one place to another,

14
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:25,090
they took their dialects with them.

15
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:29,480
So people from the mountains in the southeastern USA speak

16
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:34,040
with almost the same dialect as people in the northwestern USA.

17
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:39,800
The USA is a large country in which many different dialects are spoken.

18
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:42,820
Although many Americans move a lot,

19
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:46,580
they still recognize and understand each other’s dialects.
